WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:14.380
Modern life is deeply attracted to the
idea of progress in the 18th century as

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.590
European societies became ever richer
and more technological, the conventional

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:24.019
view was that mankind was firmly set on
a positive trajectory from savagery and

00:00:24.019 --> 00:00:28.900
ignorance toward prosperity and
civilization. But there was at least one

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.950
eighteen century philosopher who
violently disagreed and who continues to

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:38.440
have very provocative things to say to
our own era. Jean-Jacques Rousseau was born to an

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:43.460
educated watchmaker in Geneva in 1712.
when he was 10 his father got into a

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.690
legal dispute and the family was forced
to flee Geneva. From that point on

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:51.640
Rousseau's life was marked by deep
instability and isolation. As a young man

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:56.590
who went to Paris and there was exposed
to the opulence and luxury that was the

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:01.000
order of the day in Austin regime Paris.
It was a far cry from his birthplace of

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.850
Geneva a city that was sober and deeply
opposed to luxury goods. Then one day in

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:10.670
1749 he read a copy of a newspaper, The
Mercure de France that contained an advert for

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:15.189
an essay on the subject of whether
recent advances in arts and sciences

00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:20.340
have contributed to what was called the
"purification of morals" in other words

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.080
was the world getting better?

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.000
Rousseau experienced something of an
epiphany. It struck him that civilization

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.680
and progress had not in fact improved
people. Instead they'd exacted a terrible

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:37.130
destructive influence on the morality of
human beings who had once been good.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:41.430
Rousseau took this insight and turned it
into the central thesis of what became

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:46.399
his celebrated discourse on the Arts and
Sciences. His argument was simple:

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:50.920
Individuals had once been good and happy
but as people had emerged from their pre

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:57.180
social state and join society they had
become plagued by vice and sin. In this

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.479
work and its twin, The Discourse on the
Origins and Foundations of Inequality,

00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:05.000
Rousseau went on to sketch what it would
have been like at the beginning of

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.149
history, an idyllic period that he
called "the state of nature." A long time

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:10.640
ago

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.860
when men and women lived in forests and had never entered a shop or read a

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.819
newspa per the philosopher pictured people more
easily understanding their own

00:02:17.819 --> 00:02:21.959
minds and so being drawn toward
essential features of a satisfied life,

00:02:21.959 --> 00:02:23.550
a love of a family,

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:28.670
respect for nature, an awe at the beauty of
the universe, curiosity about others and

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:33.080
a taste for music and simple
entertainments. The "state of nature" was

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:38.180
moral and guided by spontaneous pity,
empathy for others and their suffering.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:42.520
So what was it about civilization that
Rousseau thought had corrupted people

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:46.870
and led to moral degeneracy. Rousseau
claimed that the march toward

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:51.790
civilization had awakened in people and
unhealthy form of self-love, amour-propre,

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.980
he called it, something that was
artificial and centered around pride,

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:01.350
jealousy, and vanity. Rousseau argued that
this destructive form of self love had

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:05.019
emerged as people had moved into cities
and there had begun to compare

00:03:05.019 --> 00:03:08.870
themselves to others and created their
identities solely by reference to their

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:13.100
neighbors. Civilized people had stopped
thinking about what they wanted and they

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:17.730
felt and merely imitated other people,
entering into ruinous competitions for

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:22.620
status and money and losing sight of
their own sensations. Rousseau is forever

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:23.950
associated with a term

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:28.480
"noble savage" because it was his work
that describe the innocence and morality

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.340
of our ancestors and contrasted it with
modern decadence. At the time Rousseau was

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.720
writing, European Society was fascinated
by the plight the native North American

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:42.940
tribes. Reports of Indian society drawn
up in the 16th century had once

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:47.450
described the Indians as materially
simple but psychologically very rich and

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.750
interesting. Communities with small,
close-knit,

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:55.930
egalitariam, religious, playful, and martial.
However within a few decades of the

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:59.900
arrival of the Europeans the status
system of Indian society have been

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:03.799
revolutionized through contact with a
technology and luxury of European

00:04:03.799 --> 00:04:09.299
industry. Indians now longed for guns
alcohol, beads, and mirrors Rates of

00:04:09.299 --> 00:04:13.760
suicide and alcoholism had risen, communities were fracturing, and factions

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.459
were squabbling. The modern world had
ruined the lives of people who'd once

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:19.070
lived

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:23.640
happily in the "state of nature." Rousseau's
interest in natural goodness made him

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:29.230
very interested in the idea, though not
quite the reality, of children. In 1762 he

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:34.340
wrote Émile, or On Education, perhaps the
most successful book ever written about

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:40.170
how to raise children. Rousseau suggested that
children were born naturally good and

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:43.620
that the key to raising them was therefore always to prevent their corruption by

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:49.410
society. This idea was widely influential.
Parents who had before this time seen

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:53.270
their children as wicked or at best as
blank slates now viewed them as founts

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:56.760
of wisdom and tried to give them a
childhood full of play and visits to

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:02.130
forests and lakes. Rousseau became the
inventor of child-centered education. He

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.810
was also a great proponent of
breastfeeding, declaring "Let mothers

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:10.810
deign to nurse their children, morals will reform themselves, nature's sentiments

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:16.630
will be awakened in every heart and the state will be repeopled." It was, he knew,

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:20.630
a bit of hyperbole but its spurred a
wave of breastfeeding even among the

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:25.500
wealthy who had long disdained the practice.
Artists rushed to paint and honor the new

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.290
vogue for breastfeeding. Because Rousseau so 
closely valued human beings in their

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:34.800
original state, it followed that in the
novels he wrote, Rousseau also constantly

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:40.380
celebrated intense feelings rather than
great deeds or social events. In his

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:45.340
novel, Julie, written in 1761, Roussseau depicted
the excitement and anguish of an

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:49.800
upper-class women caught in a love
triangle between her sensitive tutor and

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:53.830
her boring but socially sanctioned
aristocratic match. Rousseau's

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:57.920
contemporaries might have seen Julie as
unwise and her feelings as a passing

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:03.750
fancy, but Rousseau painted her love in a higher light. He urged us to see its grandeur,

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:07.520
depth and honor. In his writings about
his own life,

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:13.050
Rousseau was similarly romantic or, what one
might unkindly call, self-absorbed. In his

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:17.430
famous Confessions, one of the first ever
autobiographies, Rousseau spend pages

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:21.970
exploring his inner life: How frustrating
he found shopping, the surprising feeling of

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:26.260
tenderness for his ex's new partner, or
the joys of gardening.To him,

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:30.100
these weren't trivial or self-absorbed
topics, they were part of an important

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:30.870
task: to

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:36.240
show is like on the inside. "I have
conceived of a new genre of service to

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.820
render to man," he boasted, "to offer them
the faithful image of one amongst them

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:47.760
in order for them to learn to know
themselves. Rousseau died in 1778 age 66. His

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.440
reputation has continued to grow. He was
from beyond the grave

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:55.020
one of the heroes of the French
Fevolution and he became an icon to a

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:59.700
great many artists and writers of the
19th century. Rousseau can be considered as

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:04.060
one of the founding figures of what we
now know as the Romantic Movement, an

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:08.820
ideology responsible for valuing the
primitive over the civilized, the child

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:14.690
over the adult&lt; the passionate lover over
the calmly loyal spouse. The modern world

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:19.200
despite its addiction to status,
machinery, and capitalist values, in many

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.380
ways continues to be profoundly romantic
in its heart. It's astonishing that so

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:28.720
much of what we take to be common sense,
or just natural, can directly be traced

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:33.590
back to the work of one not always wise
but always highly intriguing and

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:34.520
provocative thinker.

